# Project Rules and Context

## Project Overview
Mahjong application with AI-powered move suggestions via REST API and web-based visualization.
- **Primary Use Case**: FF14 plugin integration for Japanese Riichi Mahjong move suggestions
- **Core Features**: 
  - REST API endpoint accepting game state, returning optimal move suggestions
  - Localhost web interface for visualizing game states
  - Game history and API call tracking
- **Full Design**: See docs/application-design.md for complete architecture

## Coding Standards
- Follow common design patterns (MVC, Repository, Service layers, etc.)
- Choose appropriate frameworks (Spring Boot for REST API, etc.)
- Include good logging throughout the application
- Use Java naming conventions and best practices
- Write unit tests for core game logic

## Architecture Notes
- **Mahjong Variant**: Japanese Riichi (primary focus)
- **AI Complexity**: Basic shanten minimization (advanced strategies deferred)
- **Multiplayer**: Not implemented initially
- **API Rate Limiting**: ~5 requests/second (adjust based on performance)
- **Persistence**: Store game history and API call logs; no user accounts for now
- **Technology Stack**: Java + Spring Boot backend, React/Vue.js or vanilla JS frontend

## Important Conventions
- API responses should include move suggestions with confidence scores and reasoning
- Game state serialization uses JSON format
- Visualization should clearly highlight suggested moves
- Log all API calls for debugging and history tracking


